60:**`compile` is defined as the composition of the stage functions listed in `Stages.lean`**, so a pass that isn't registered can't be in the pipeline. `Output` is pure data with `DecidableEq`: `{ toks, ast, p, sites, tokens, mir, mem, bir, ll, lcb }`. There are no proofs inside it, and every theorem is stated *about* `compile`.
64:**CheckedCore is the single admission point.** Trap admission, termination and fact derivation run there, and nowhere else. The checker also emits one **discharged-evidence token** per operation it proved safe; in slice 0 that is `known (v)` for a `+` whose value is statically known. Tokens are carried through MIR, Memory CFG and BIR. A token is validated (`token_valid`). Its soundness connects the checker's known value to the run-time operands through `knownExpr_sound`, because `knownExpr_u8` alone establishes only static knowledge. Only a validated token lets a pass omit a check. No pass removes a check without evidence (PASS-9).
66:**Place MIR** is where later slices put evaluation order, cleanup edges and the expansion of residual checks. In slice 0, an add with no valid token becomes an explicit `add.ovf` plus a branch to a trap site, and an add with a valid token becomes a plain add. Every accepted slice-0 program has tokens on all its adds, so its generated IR (MIR through BIR and both backend inputs) contains no trap path. Whether the executable contains trap code is an artifact fact, not established yet. Unchecked well-scoped programs, such as `200 + 56` in gate 6, have no tokens, so the check-and-trap path is still proved and tested.
68:**Memory CFG** is the one layout authority. It consumes the spec's byte encoding (`ConcreteSpec/Spec/Rep.lean`) and turns places into loads and stores over block-offset memory with provenance. In slice 0 every local is a `u8` slot (size 1, alignment 1), so the pass is identity-shaped. It exists from day one so that layout never leaks into SSA construction or the printers.
70:**Typed source identity, end to end.** The lexer and parser mint typed `SiteId`s and spans. Elaboration records an origin edge for every generated temporary in `out.sites`. Every MIR, Memory CFG and BIR instruction carries its origin, and so does every trap site. Diagnostics, facts, JSON and certificates all read this one evidence graph. Core itself has no positions yet (spec open items O1/O4), so the site map sits beside `out.p` without changing the spec's Core.
