K13 has the right direction, but understates the adopted design in several places. The strongest long-term solution is a typed evidence graph with precise explanations and independent-error reporting, established from slice 0. These are design recommendations; no files were modified.

Each verdict below addresses the corresponding point in [_review/K13.md](_review/K13.md).

- **a — AGREE, with required additions.** One typed record with several renderers is already decided in [the diagnostics row](docs/decisions.md#decided-project-diagnostics-2026-11-06) and planned in [R-0593](ROADMAP.md:461). K13’s field list omits adopted essentials: typed subject, refusal kind, evidence, expected/actual, policy origin and blocked dependencies. Preserve those structurally; never reconstruct them from prose. R-0603 requires individual rule revisions and retired IDs, without numeric codes.

- **b — AGREE.** End-to-end provenance is already planned in [MERGED item 5](docs/proposals/MERGED.md:62) and [the slice-0 design](Concrete/README.md:70). Type origins need causal edges, beyond locations. Store canonical byte ranges against identified source snapshots; derive line/column views rather than maintaining duplicate coordinates. Preserve multiple origins through lowering, specialization and cleanup. Byte columns and tabs counting one byte are decided in [the runtime row](docs/decisions.md#decided-project-runtime-2026-11-06).

- **c — DISAGREE as framed.** The adopted [diagnostics proposal, §7](docs/archive/crucial-2026-11-06/DIAGNOSTICS.md) already specifies `concrete explain <rule>`; it is not a fresh undecided command. Keep that single explanation entry point, displaying the exact revision’s doc comment, Lean statement and checked examples. Do not generate another prose account: [PHILOSOPHY rules 5–6](docs/PHILOSOPHY.md) distinguish derived views from duplicated specification.

- **d — AGREE, with precise limits.** Plain language, source excerpts and actionable explanations are sound. However, “certainly correct” must mean the adopted [fix contract](docs/archive/crucial-2026-11-06/DIAGNOSTICS.md): whole-program acceptance after editing, explicit status, and discharged lemma conditions before automatic application. Acceptance alone does not establish intended behaviour. When no qualifying fix exists, explain the failed premise and offer examples; never invent an edit to satisfy the style guide.

- **e — DISAGREE.** First-error-per-file is a shortcut: it hides independent errors and still allows cross-file cascades. The adopted [§5 cascade rule](docs/archive/crucial-2026-11-06/DIAGNOSTICS.md) is stronger: dependent judgments become blocked results. Report every independently justified root refusal, deduplicate by typed subject/rule, and order deterministically. Continue only where prerequisites remain valid; recovery must never turn missing evidence into acceptance.

- **f — DISAGREE as a sufficient gate.** Goldens are already required by [R-0593](ROADMAP.md:461); model scoring is additional, fallible review. A reviewed snapshot can preserve an incorrect diagnosis. Require checker/refusal correspondence, exact spans, blocked-dependency tests, renderer agreement and rechecked fixes alongside goldens. Use model review to identify wording defects, with a versioned rubric and recorded findings, rather than treating its score as correctness evidence.

The concrete lessons from the other compilers are:

| Compiler | Take | Do not take |
|---|---|---|
| **Elm** | Context-sensitive expected/actual explanations: its error representation distinguishes expectations from annotations and expression contexts. | Its heuristic conversion hints as evidence of the intended repair. Concrete should offer an edit only under its stronger fix contract. [Elm implementation](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/elm/compiler/master/compiler/src/Reporting/Error/Type.hs) |
| **rustc** | Labelled primary/secondary spans, structured suggestions, explicit applicability and extended explanations. | A parallel numeric error-code namespace or treating applicability as meaning preservation. Concrete already has revisioned Lean identities. [rustc diagnostics guide](https://rustc-dev-guide.rust-lang.org/diagnostics.html) |
| **Gleam** | Construct-specific explanations and continued analysis of independent definitions, preserving useful editor information. | First-error stopping: Gleam documents how that approach hid genuine errors and left language-server information stale. Definition-level recovery is useful, but Concrete should track actual dependencies. [Fault tolerant Gleam](https://gleam.run/news/fault-tolerant-gleam/) |
| **Roc** | Continuing useful independent analysis and suppressing secondary compile-time failures caused by an existing error. | Running programs containing static errors, with invalid paths becoming runtime crashes. Concrete’s admission boundary must remain intact. [Roc compile-time evaluation](https://www.roc-lang.org/docs/main/langref/compile-time/), [tutorial](https://roc-lang.org/tutorial/) |

For **c**, I recommend **`concrete explain <rule>`**, accepting the full revisioned identity. Every diagnostic should show the exact invocation. The browsable owner view can expose the same authoritative material for inspection; it should not require a separately maintained explanation. Preserve historical revision content so an old diagnostic never silently receives today’s changed explanation.

For **e**, the long-term rule is **all independent root refusals; dependent judgments explicitly blocked**. For example, a failed declaration must not produce “unknown member” refusals in every consumer, while an unrelated arithmetic refusal in the same file should still appear. Parser recovery may continue at sound synchronization boundaries, but fabricated tokens or error placeholders must remain marked and cannot justify semantic claims. Any output cap must explicitly report truncation.

K1 must carry more than “one span and stable ID per node” in [_review/KLIST.md](_review/KLIST.md):

- Typed source-file and snapshot identities, content digests, canonical half-open byte ranges, and defined EOF/empty-span behaviour.
- Distinct source-site, semantic-subject and generated-instance identities; spans are locations, not identities.
- Typed origin edges for desugaring, temporaries, specialization, cleanup, duplicated and merged instructions, with explicit synthetic origins.
- Type/evidence causes: which annotation, binding, argument or premise established an expectation, plus dependencies needed to distinguish root refusals from blocked judgments.
- Checked mappings through every transformation to trap sites and final artifact locations, retaining relevant call/definition origins rather than choosing an arbitrary span.
- A defined identity lifetime across edits and builds, including how fix rechecking matches subjects after spans move.

**Yes, this must start before slice 1—at the first front-end output.** [R-0593](ROADMAP.md:461) already requires that timing, [R-0575](ROADMAP.md:458) requires source-located facts from slice 0, and [Concrete/README.md](Concrete/README.md:23) already plans tokens with spans. The full machinery grows with later stages, but typed interfaces and preservation obligations must precede their consumers. A separate Core site map is sound if its association and preservation are checked; an unchecked positional side table would be a shortcut.

Additional findings:

- **K13 drops epistemic distinctions.** The adopted result distinguishes acceptance, refusal and incompleteness, and distinguishes a false premise from evidence not established. “Cannot prove safe” must never become “will overflow.”
- **Coordinate presentation needs a contract.** Keep the decided byte columns, but render Unicode and tabs accurately. Test malformed input, multiline spans, EOF and terminal-control characters.
- **Provenance needs correctness gates.** Test origin preservation under specialization, cleanup, duplication and merging. Goldens alone cannot establish that a plausible highlighted span is the correct source cause.
- **Versioning has several independent dimensions.** Schema version, `spec_version`, rule revision and source/artifact identity must remain distinct. [R-0603](ROADMAP.md:415) explicitly separates rule revisions from the output’s spec-version header.
- **Avoid overclaiming current implementation.** [Concrete/README.md](Concrete/README.md:1) is explicitly provisional. Planned provenance and proof obligations are not evidence that every stage already implements them.

ANSWER: AGREE=a,b,d; DISAGREE=c,e,f; EXPLAIN=command; CASCADE=independent roots, dependents blocked; BEFORE_SLICE1=yes; MISSED=result distinctions, snapshot identity, provenance laws, Unicode rendering, versioning
